### Description

When an allocation fails while `json::parse()` builds an object or array member, the result is destroyed with a member whose type says *object*, *array* or *string* but whose pointer is null. The destructor then dereferences that null pointer: UBSan reports `reference binding to null pointer`, a debug build fails `assert_invariant()`, a release build segfaults. The `std::bad_alloc` never reaches the caller, although [`parse()` documents the strong guarantee](https://json.nlohmann.me/api/basic_json/parse/#exception-safety).

**Root cause.** The DOM SAX parser constructs a new member *in place* over the existing null member it inserted for the key ([json_sax.hpp#L1300-L1313](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/detail/input/json_sax.hpp#L1300-L1313), the same code in `json_sax_dom_callback_parser`):

```cpp
if (JSON_HEDLEY_LIKELY(member.is_null()))
{
    // a new member is null and holds nothing: construct the value
    // over it instead of destroying and swapping
    ::new (static_cast<void*>(&member)) BasicJsonType(std::forward<Value>(v));
}
```

`basic_json(value_t)` initialises `data::m_type` before `data::m_value` ([json.hpp#L6216-L6219](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L6216-L6219)), and `json_value(value_t)` / `json_value(string_t&&)` allocate. When that allocation throws, the constructor never completes, so no destructor runs for it, but the placement new has already written `m_type = object` (or array/string) into the member's storage, while the union still holds the null member's zero pointer. The member is a fully constructed element of its parent's container, so the parent's destructor destroys it: `destroy_bounded()` dereferences the null pointer at [json.hpp#L912](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L912) (object) or [#L902](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L902) (array).

The two other branches of `set_member()` (a repeated key, or a custom `json_base_class_t`) assign `member = BasicJsonType(...)` and are safe. The binary readers use `json_sax_dom_binary_parser`, which assigns a temporary ([json_sax.hpp#L1598](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/detail/input/json_sax.hpp#L1598)); the same fail-point sweep over `from_cbor`, `from_msgpack`, `from_bson` and `from_ubjson` found no problem there.

**Also affected.** `ordered_json` (same code), builds with `JSON_DIAGNOSTICS` (array member, line 902), and `sax_parse()` with `detail::json_sax_dom_parser` into an existing value, which is left holding the invalid member.

**Since when.** Introduced by #5882 (9c50bdd140, 2026-10-24, "Make the DOM parser cheaper per container and per member"), which added the placement-new path. Not in any release: the same program runs to completion with the 3.12.0 header (every allocation point throws cleanly).

**Possible fix.** Keep the placement new only where the constructor cannot throw (scalars), and for owning kinds construct the value first and move it into the member, as the binary parser does:

```cpp
member = BasicJsonType(std::forward<Value>(v));   // or: construct a local, then placement-new by move (noexcept)
```

Alternatively, allocate the `json_value` first and set `m_type` only afterwards (the pattern #5585 established for the other constructors). A regression test belongs in `tests/src/unit-allocator.cpp`: run `parse()` of an object with nested containers and strings with a global or custom allocator that fails at every allocation in turn, and check that every failure is a clean `std::bad_alloc` and that nothing leaks.

### Reproduction steps

1. Save the program below as `parse_badalloc.cpp`.
2. `clang++ -std=c++17 -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined -I include parse_badalloc.cpp -o parse_badalloc && ./parse_badalloc`

The program replaces `operator new` so that the `k`-th allocation throws `std::bad_alloc`, and runs `json::parse()` for k = 1, 2, 3, …

### Expected vs. actual results

Expected: every iteration prints `k=… threw bad_alloc, fine` until no allocation fails any more (`no throw at k=…: done`).

Actual: k = 1 … 10 are fine; at k = 11 the program dies in `~basic_json()` while `parse_impl()` unwinds (see *Error messages*). GCC 16 / libstdc++ in Docker behaves the same (`json.hpp:912:17: runtime error: reference binding to null pointer of type 'struct map'`).

### Minimal code example

```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <new>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
using json = nlohmann::json;
static long counter = 0, fail_at = 0;
void* operator new(std::size_t n)
{
    if (fail_at > 0 && ++counter == fail_at) throw std::bad_alloc();
    void* p = std::malloc(n ? n : 1); if (!p) throw std::bad_alloc(); return p;
}
void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
int main()
{
    const char* text = R"({"a":"a long string that does not fit into the small string buffer1","b":[1,2],"c":{"x":1},"d":1})";
    for (long k = 1; k < 1000; ++k)
    {
        counter = 0; fail_at = k;
        try { json j = json::parse(text); fail_at = 0; std::printf("no throw at k=%ld: done\n", k); return 0; }
        catch (const std::bad_alloc&) { fail_at = 0; std::printf("k=%ld threw bad_alloc, fine\n", k); }
    }
}
```

### Error messages

```
k=1 threw bad_alloc, fine
…
k=10 threw bad_alloc, fine
include/nlohmann/json.hpp:912:36: runtime error: reference binding to null pointer of type 'object_t' (aka 'map<std::string, nlohmann::basic_json<…>>')
    #0 basic_json<>::json_value::destroy_bounded(basic_json<>&, unsigned long) json.hpp:912
    #1 basic_json<>::json_value::destroy_bounded(basic_json<>&, unsigned long) json.hpp:916
    #2 basic_json<>::json_value::destroy_container(detail::value_t) json.hpp:885
    #3 basic_json<>::json_value::destroy_owned(detail::value_t) json.hpp:1034
    #4 basic_json<>::json_value::destroy(detail::value_t) json.hpp:1016
    #5 basic_json<>::data::~data() json.hpp:6235
    #7 basic_json<>::~basic_json() json.hpp:2723
    #9 basic_json<>::parse_impl<…>(…) json.hpp:3111
    #10 basic_json<>::parse<char const*&>(…) json.hpp:6025
    #11 main parse_badalloc.cpp:21
SUMMARY: UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer: undefined-behavior include/nlohmann/json.hpp:912:36
```

The allocation that fails at k = 11 is `create<object_t>()` for the member `"c"`, called from `json_value::init_owned()` ← `json_value(value_t)` ← `data(value_t)` ← `basic_json(value_t)` ← `json_sax_dom_parser::set_member()`.

### Compiler and operating system

Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.3.34.2), libc++, macOS 27; GCC 16.1, libstdc++, Debian (Docker `gcc:16`).

### Library version

develop at ddc34f546a (2026-11-01); 3.12.0 is not affected.

### Validation

- [x] The bug also occurs if the latest version from the [`develop`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/tree/develop) branch is used.
- [x] I can successfully [compile and run the unit tests](https://github.com/nlohmann/json#execute-unit-tests).

---
*Found by an allocation fail-point sweep during a bug hunt; written by Claude Code on behalf of @nlohmann.*

